Error-logging test configuration (err/)
=======================================

The 'err' test exercises the OTel filter's runtime error-reporting path: the
rate-limited, edge-triggered logging of errors and warnings, the per-instance
counters, and the admin CLI commands that report and reset them.  The scopes
deliberately fail at span creation on every response, so the error handler runs
on ordinary traffic without any fault injection.

A response scope parents its span under 'orphan span', a span that only the
on-server-unavailable scope creates.  That event never fires while the backend
is alive, so the parent is absent from the runtime context and span creation
fails with "cannot find referenced span/context".  Every response therefore
raises one runtime error.

In the soft-errors mode used here the error is swallowed at warning level and
the request still completes.  Switching the running instance to hard-errors
mode logs at error level instead and disables the filter for the stream that
raised the error.


Files
-----

  err/otel.cfg    OTel filter configuration (error-triggering scopes)
  err/haproxy.cfg HAProxy frontend/backend configuration
  err/otel.yml    Exporter, processor, reader and provider definitions


Events
------

  on-client-session-start  creates the 'HAProxy session' root span
  on-server-unavailable    creates 'orphan span', but does not fire while
                           the backend is alive
  on-http-response         parents 'HTTP response' under the absent
                           'orphan span', which fails

  The session-start scope succeeds and the server-unavailable scope never runs,
  so the response scope is the one that fails and each request raises at most
  one error episode.


Error reporting
---------------

  The error and warning logs are edge-triggered per instance and rate limited
  per sliding window; lines held back are counted and reported as a "(N more
  suppressed)" suffix on the next emitted line.  A clean callback re-arms the
  edge trigger, so steady traffic keeps reporting fresh episodes rather than
  collapsing them into one.

  The otel.cfg log directive forwards the lines to the configured syslog server,
  at warning level in soft-errors mode and at error level in hard-errors mode.
  The configuration, however, also sets 'option dontlog-normal', dropping the
  warning-level lines: in the default soft-errors mode only the counters move;
  the lines appear once the instance is switched to hard-errors mode, or once
  the option is removed.

  The admin CLI on the stats socket reports and controls them:

    flt-otel status         show the hard, soft and suppressed counts
    flt-otel hard-errors    switch to hard-errors mode (log and disable)
    flt-otel soft-errors    switch back to soft-errors mode
    flt-otel reset-errors   zero the counters and re-arm the edge trigger


Running the test
----------------

The run-err.sh script (a symlink to run-test-config.sh) runs this
configuration.  It invokes haproxy with -f haproxy-common.cfg -f
err/haproxy.cfg, the same as the manual command shown below.  From the
test/ directory:

  % ./run-err.sh

Or run it manually:

  % /path/to/haproxy -f haproxy-common.cfg -f err/haproxy.cfg

Then drive a few requests and inspect the counters:

  % curl -sS http://127.0.0.1:10080/ >/dev/null
  % echo "flt-otel status" | socat - UNIX-CONNECT:/tmp/haproxy.sock

In the default soft-errors mode the requests only move the counters.  To read
the log lines themselves, switch the instance to hard-errors mode first, and
mind the rate limiter, which holds the output to a few lines per window:

  % echo "flt-otel hard-errors" | socat - UNIX-CONNECT:/tmp/haproxy.sock
  % curl -sS http://127.0.0.1:10080/ >/dev/null
